FILE: context/source-hints/001-README.md
     1	# write-uuter
     2
     3	`write-uuter` is a Go CLI that turns a Markdown brief into a reviewed article
     4	while preserving the evidence, outline, candidates, reviews, and PM decisions
     5	that produced it.
     6
     7	## Requirements
     8
     9	- Go 1.26 or later to build
    10	- tmux on the host
    11	- an installed and authenticated Codex CLI for real runs
    12	- macOS for real runs; the controller uses the native Seatbelt sandbox to
    13	  enforce role filesystem isolation
    14
    15	No third-party Go packages are used.
    16
    17	Linux cross-builds are supported, but Linux execution fails closed until an
    18	equivalent native read-isolation backend is implemented.
    19
    20	## Build and test
    21
    22	```sh
    23	make build
    24	make verify
    25	```
    26
    27	The tests build the CLI and execute it as a subprocess against a deterministic
    28	fake Codex executable inside real tmux sessions. They do not require network
    29	access or Codex authentication.
    30
    31	## Run
    32
    33	From the repository root:
    34
    35	```sh
    36	./bin/write-uuter run \
    37	  --brief examples/brief.md \
    38	  --run-dir smoke-runs/my-run
    39	```
    40
    41	The run directory must not already exist. Its parent is created when needed.
    42	On success the command exits 0 and `smoke-runs/my-run/article.md` is a non-empty
    43	exact copy of the candidate accepted by all four reviewers. A runtime failure,
    44	timeout, exhausted third candidate, or need for human judgment exits non-zero
    45	and preserves a `workflow.json` whose status is `blocked`. Invalid briefs and
    46	existing targets exit non-zero without creating or changing a run.
    47
    48	Operational options:
    49
    50	```text
    51	--codex <path>       Codex executable (default: codex)
    52	--tmux <path>        tmux executable (default: tmux)
    53	--timeout <duration> Per-agent artifact timeout (default: 10m)
    54	--prompts-dir <path> Checked-in prompt directory; an explicit value is binding
    55	```
    56
    57	The brief requires these case-insensitive level-two headings; all except
    58	`Source hints` need non-whitespace content:
    59
    60	```text
    61	Question, Audience, Provisional takeaway, Scope, Out of scope,
    62	Publication target, Constraints, Done when, Source hints
    63	```
    64
    65	Relative source-hint paths are resolved from the directory containing the
    66	input brief.
    67
    68	## Runtime model
    69
    70	Go owns state transitions, validation, revision hashes, timeouts, and process
    71	cleanup. One long-lived PM Codex process and at most one worker run in a
    72	dedicated tmux session. Each process receives a separate controller-created
    73	workspace outside the durable run directory; Go copies in only the role's
    74	contracted context and copies validated regular-file outputs back. A native
    75	sandbox denies agents access to the durable run, other role workspaces, and
    76	controller-private launch state. Codex's inner sandbox is disabled because
    77	macOS does not support nesting it inside the stricter controller sandbox. Go
    78	owns each invocation and its descendants with a unique identity token, requires
    79	a pre-launch ready handshake, and publishes completion through an atomic marker
    80	only after descendants are gone.
    81	Researcher, Story Editor, Writer, then fresh Evidence, Story, Clarity, and Copy
    82	reviewer processes run sequentially. Reviewers never receive the run directory
    83	or edit candidates. Only PM-validated must-fix findings create a new candidate;
    84	candidate 003 is the hard limit.
    85
    86	See [workflow](docs/workflow.md), [roles](docs/roles.md), and
    87	[artifacts](docs/artifacts.md) for the exact shipped contracts.
FILE: context/source-hints/002-workflow.md
     1	# Workflow
     2
     3	## Controller sequence
     4
     5	The issue-1 implementation is a single-run, non-resumable controller. It first
     6	parses every required level-two brief section and verifies that the target does
     7	not exist. It builds the initial workspace in a temporary sibling directory
     8	and commits that directory with an operating-system no-replace rename only
     9	after initialization succeeds. A concurrently created directory or symlink is
    10	never replaced.
    11
    12	```mermaid
    13	flowchart TD
    14	    v[Validate brief and new target] --> i[Atomically initialize run]
    15	    i --> pm[Start persistent PM in tmux]
    16	    pm --> r[Researcher]
    17	    r --> s[Story Editor]
    18	    s --> w[Writer creates candidate 001]
    19	    w --> e[Fresh Evidence Reviewer]
    20	    e --> pe[PM classifies findings]
    21	    pe -->|no must-fix| sr[Fresh Story Reviewer]
    22	    sr --> ps[PM classifies findings]
    23	    ps -->|no must-fix| c[Fresh Clarity Reviewer]
    24	    c --> pc[PM classifies findings]
    25	    pc -->|no must-fix| cp[Fresh Copy Reviewer]
    26	    cp --> pp[PM classifies findings]
    27	    pp -->|all gates pass| f[Exact-copy candidate to article.md]
    28	    pe -->|validated must-fix| b{Candidate below 003?}
    29	    ps -->|validated must-fix| b
    30	    pc -->|validated must-fix| b
    31	    pp -->|validated must-fix| b
    32	    b -->|yes| rw[Writer creates next candidate]
    33	    rw --> e
    34	    b -->|no| blocked[Block and preserve artifacts]
    35	    pe -->|human judgment| blocked
    36	    ps -->|human judgment| blocked
    37	    pc -->|human judgment| blocked
    38	    pp -->|human judgment| blocked
    39	```
    40
    41	Review lenses are never parallel. A must-fix stops the remaining lenses for
    42	that candidate and the replacement restarts at Evidence. Optional and invalid
    43	findings do not consume a candidate. A human-judgment decision blocks.
    44
    45	## Artifact gates
    46
    47	Go does not treat an agent's final message or process exit as completion. A
    48	worker must exit successfully before Go reads its owned files, and may advance
    49	only when those files exist and pass validation:
    50
    51	- research has non-empty sources and a claim ledger naming Fact, Firsthand
    52	  observation, Inference, Opinion, and Unresolved;
    53	- the outline records Purpose, Supporting evidence, and Reader takeaway;
    54	- each candidate is non-empty and has no TODO placeholder;
    55	- reviewer JSON has an allowed status, exact lens and SHA-256 revision, unique
    56	  complete findings, plus a report with the same finding fields;
    57	- PM decisions cover every finding, use allowed classifications, match the
    58	  current revision, explain every invalid classification, and preserve the
    59	  exact accepted classifications and routing outcome of every earlier lens.
    60
    61	A reviewer changing its candidate is an artifact-contract failure. Stale lens
    62	or revision metadata is rejected rather than retried into a passing state.
    63
    64	## Lifecycle and terminal states
    65
    66	The PM starts before research, atomically publishes its protocol-ready marker,
    67	and remains active while Go starts one worker window at a time. Go verifies
    68	both the PM Codex process identity and this application-level handshake before
    69	starting any worker, and rechecks PM liveness before accepting worker output.
    70	Each role runs in a fresh private workspace outside the run
    71	directory. Go stages only its allowed inputs, waits for a natural successful
    72	exit marker and tmux-window disappearance, validates output without following
    73	symlinks, and then copies regular files into the run. The marker is published
    74	by a same-directory temporary-file rename only after the Codex process and its
    75	descendants are gone. The controller-private runner, ancestry ownership
    76	manifests/ready records,
    77	live PM requests, and other launch-critical state are siblings of—not children
    78	of—agent workspaces. A default-deny macOS native sandbox gives agents only
    79	system runtime reads plus their current workspace and isolated Codex home; it
    80	denies unrelated host, durable-run, prior-lens, PM, and controller-private
    81	access. Controller-only test paths never become sandbox rules or agent
    82	environment variables. The private runner follows parent/child edges through
    83	the native process table and durably records precise kernel process identities
    84	for the invocation ancestry, including children that create a new session or
    85	process group, clear their environment, or execute a protected system binary.
    86	Cleanup opens stable kernel process handles before signaling each recorded
    87	identity, so a reused bare PID is never treated as owned. Each lens uses a
    88	fresh Codex invocation.
    89
    90	Every agent has the configured timeout, and tmux lifecycle commands have their
    91	own short bound. The controller enforces both a context timer and an absolute
    92	wall-clock deadline, so host sleep or a missing runner completion marker cannot
    93	extend an invocation past its contract. A timeout, premature exit, malformed
    94	artifact, stale review,
    95	cleanup failure, human decision, or exhausted candidate budget sets an
    96	actionable `workflow.json.block_reason`. Go verifies that the dedicated tmux
    97	session and every unresolved invocation identity are gone before either
    98	terminal result. On success it requires the persistent PM to still be live,
    99	then revalidates the candidate hash, every final review, each PM request
   100	binding, and each accepted classification list before publishing `article.md`
   101	and durably persisting the succeeded state. A failure during that terminal
   102	transition removes `article.md`, records blocked state, attempts private-state
   103	cleanup even if blocked-state persistence fails, and leaves no PM, worker, or
   104	detached descendant process for the run.
   105
   106	Parallel runs, resume after controller restart, and editing completed runs are
   107	not implemented.
FILE: context/source-hints/003-roles.md
     1	# Roles
     2
     3	Role instructions are durable files under `prompts/`. Generated assignments
     4	are created in a controller-private runtime outside the run and combine those
     5	instructions with the allowed artifact context. They are copied to the run's
     6	`.control/prompts/` only after all processes are stopped. Go validates role
     7	output and owns every workflow transition. The durable prompt set includes the
     8	PM polling protocol and the shared reviewer output/filesystem contract; those
     9	protocols are not embedded as Go string literals.
    10
    11	## Human Editor
    12
    13	The human owns `brief.md` and resolves decisions classified as
    14	`needs_human_judgment`. Issue 1 does not resume a blocked run; the preserved
    15	artifacts support a later inspected retry in a new run directory.
    16
    17	## PM
    18
    19	The PM is one long-lived Codex process in its own isolated workspace. It watches
    20	private, request-ID-specific review requests; Go validates and records the
    21	result as `pm-decisions/article-00N.md`. It classifies every finding as:
    22
    23	- `valid_must_fix`
    24	- `valid_optional`
    25	- `invalid` with a non-empty reason
    26	- `needs_human_judgment`
    27
    28	The PM never writes a candidate or review. Each decision record must repeat the
    29	active request ID and a digest of that review, preserve and revalidate every
    30	previously reached lens without changing its accepted classification list, and
    31	contain no future lens. The PM response must be exactly one complete fenced
    32	JSON document. Go independently validates the decision file and applies routing
    33	and the three-candidate limit. Human judgment takes precedence if a response
    34	also contains a must-fix decision. A response is accepted only while the
    35	persistent PM process, tmux window, and ready/ownership identity are all live.
    36
    37	## Researcher
    38
    39	The Researcher owns `evidence/sources.md`, optional
    40	`evidence/firsthand.md`/assets, and `claim-ledger.md`. The ledger distinguishes
    41	fact, firsthand observation, inference, opinion, and unresolved claims.
    42
    43	## Story Editor
    44
    45	The Story Editor owns `outline.md`. Every planned section records purpose,
    46	supporting evidence, and reader takeaway.
    47
    48	## Writer
    49
    50	The Writer owns exactly one assigned `drafts/article-00N.md`. The initial
    51	assignment uses the brief, evidence, ledger, and outline. A revision also gets
    52	the prior candidate, its PM decision, and the reached review result/report so
    53	the validated finding's exact problem and suggested direction are available.
    54	The Writer cannot classify findings, change earlier candidates, or create
    55	`article.md`.
    56
    57	## Reviewers
    58
    59	Evidence, Story, Clarity, and Copy reviewers run in that order as four fresh,
    60	sequential Codex processes. They never edit candidates. Every reviewer gets
    61	only its durable lens prompt, full brief, exact candidate, and candidate
    62	revision, plus:
    63
    64	| Lens | Additional context |
    65	| --- | --- |
    66	| Evidence | sources, optional firsthand evidence, claim ledger |
    67	| Story | outline |
    68	| Clarity | extracted audience and constraints |
    69	| Copy | optional repository style guide |
    70
    71	Each owns `reviews/article-00N/<lens>/result.json` and `report.md`. The report
    72	must contain one complete five-field entry for every JSON finding in the same
    73	order. The process's filesystem contains only the listed inputs under
    74	`context/` and its output root. Default-deny macOS Seatbelt enforcement denies
    75	unrelated host paths as well as reads of the
    76	durable run and prior-lens/PM/controller workspaces. The
    77	controller uses this external boundary instead of nesting Codex's own sandbox,
    78	which macOS does not permit. This
    79	implementation omits prior-lens conversation and reports from reviewer
    80	assignments; a reviewer process has no inherited conversation from another
    81	lens.
FILE: context/source-hints/004-artifacts.md
     1	# Artifacts
     2
     3	## Run layout
     4
     5	```text
     6	<run-dir>/
     7	├── brief.md
     8	├── workflow.json
     9	├── evidence/
    10	│   ├── sources.md
    11	│   ├── firsthand.md                 # optional
    12	│   └── assets/
    13	├── claim-ledger.md
    14	├── outline.md
    15	├── drafts/
    16	│   ├── article-001.md
    17	│   └── article-00N.md
    18	├── reviews/
    19	│   └── article-00N/
    20	│       ├── evidence/{result.json,report.md}
    21	│       ├── story/{result.json,report.md}
    22	│       ├── clarity/{result.json,report.md}
    23	│       └── copy/{result.json,report.md}
    24	├── pm-decisions/
    25	│   └── article-00N.md
    26	├── article.md                        # success only
    27	└── .control/                         # post-cleanup audit copies
    28	```
    29
    30	Earlier candidates, partial lens sequences, reviews, and PM decisions are kept
    31	when a revision occurs or a run blocks. `article.md` is written only after all
    32	four final-candidate lenses pass PM routing and is byte-for-byte identical to
    33	that candidate.
    34
    35	## Review result
    36
    37	`result.json` has this validated minimum shape:
    38
    39	```json
    40	{
    41	  "status": "fix_required",
    42	  "lens": "clarity",
    43	  "reviewed_revision": "sha256:...",
    44	  "findings": [
    45	    {
    46	      "id": "clarity-001",
    47	      "severity": "must_fix",
    48	      "location": "section: Introduction",
    49	      "problem": "The requested action is not visible.",
    50	      "suggested_direction": "State the action before the background."
    51	    }
    52	  ]
    53	}
    54	```
    55
    56	Allowed statuses are `clean`, `fix_required`, and `blocked`. A clean result has
    57	no findings; `fix_required` has at least one. IDs are non-empty and unique
    58	within the result, all finding fields contain non-whitespace text,
    59	lens/revision must match the assignment, and `report.md` contains one exact,
    60	complete five-field entry per JSON finding in the same order.
    61	The controller rejects unknown fields and duplicate object keys recursively,
    62	including keys inside every finding object; JSON's usual last-value-wins
    63	behavior is never used for routing artifacts.
    64
    65	## PM decision
    66
    67	`pm-decisions/article-00N.md` contains one fenced JSON object. Its `lenses` map
    68	accumulates only the lenses reached for that candidate:
    69
    70	```json
    71	{
    72	  "reviewed_revision": "sha256:...",
    73	  "lenses": {
    74	    "evidence": {
    75	      "request_id": "request-token",
    76	      "review_digest": "sha256:...",
    77	      "decisions": []
    78	    },
    79	    "story": {
    80	      "request_id": "request-token",
    81	      "review_digest": "sha256:...",
    82	      "decisions": [
    83	        {
    84	          "finding_id": "story-001",
    85	          "decision": "invalid",
    86	          "reason": "The candidate already satisfies the outline."
    87	        }
    88	      ]
    89	    }
    90	  }
    91	}
    92	```
    93
    94	Each reached lens covers every finding exactly once. Unknown, duplicate, or
    95	missing IDs, stale revisions, mismatched request IDs or review digests, dropped
    96	prior lenses, changed prior classification lists, changed prior routing
    97	outcomes, multiple fenced documents, and prepopulated future lenses fail the
    98	contract.
    99	Unknown fields and duplicate keys are rejected recursively in the top-level
   100	document, each lens record, and every decision object.
   101
   102	## workflow.json
   103
   104	`workflow.json` is atomically rewritten and is the controller's source of
   105	truth. Schema version 1 records:
   106
   107	- `status`: `running`, `succeeded`, or `blocked`;
   108	- `phase`: current controller phase;
   109	- `current_candidate` and `current_revision` (`sha256:<hex>`);
   110	- `active_role`;
   111	- stable relative `artifact_paths`;
   112	- `review_attempt_count` (one per reviewer process);
   113	- `started_at`, `updated_at`, and terminal `completed_at` timestamps;
   114	- terminal `block_reason` when blocked.
   115
   116	The durable `.control/` directory is controller-owned and preserves audit
   117	copies of generated prompt assignments, per-invocation logs, and exit
   118	markers. The live runner executable, sandbox profiles, ready/ownership records,
   119	PM requests, and agent workspaces exist only in a controller-private sibling
   120	directory. Worker `0` markers record natural successful exits. The persistent
   121	PM is controller-terminated after final validation, so its durable `143`
   122	marker is explicitly controller-synthesized cleanup evidence rather than a
   123	natural PM exit; interrupted workers likewise retain a nonzero terminal marker.
   124	Private state is removed after verified tmux/process-ownership cleanup and is
   125	never copied into `.control/`. Completion markers are atomically renamed into
   126	place; a partial marker cannot advance the workflow. Controller artifact access
   127	rejects symlink path components, symlinked tree roots/directories, and
   128	non-regular files. Editorial completion never depends on tmux scrollback or
   129	chat transcripts.

